  {
    "episode_id": 83221,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83221.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely not. Absolutely not. I know their leaders are hungering for world recognition. But all of their fighters on the ground, have they changed? No, we're still hearing stories about them going from door to door. knocking on homes, trying to find those that were allied with the American forces, threatening to kill them, their family members. We saw a woman that was beaten to death in the streets the other day by the Taliban. If that's allowing women additional freedom, I hate to see it across Afghanistan. We have a lot of women that we are very, very concerned about in the country. Those that are now educated, those that have risen into leadership positions and positions of authority. And we know that they will be a target of the Taliban. There is no way the Taliban will allow strong women leaders to exist within Afghanistan. It truly is a threat to their overall organization and what they consider their religious code.",
