  {
    "episode_id": 83221,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83221.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 39,
    "turn_text": "Yes, and it's hard to account for all of it, because again, with such a hazard with the draw, I don't know that we have inventories of exactly what was left behind at locations or what was turned over to the Afghan national military system. So we don't know what is coming from Afghan security forces that maybe turned over their weapons to the Taliban as they surrendered or dissipated. We don't know what was left behind at maybe Bagram Air Base. So what we understood is that all of this military equipment was being retrograded back to the United States or into other nations. So we see an awful lot of equipment that has been left behind, which is now in the Taliban's hands. And we know now, especially if it's sensitive equipment, it can be obtained by other violent extremist organizations It can be obtained by or near peer competitors like China and Russia. And then they can further reverse engineer and understand our equipment and how they can fight against this type of equipment. So it is a huge concern and a horrible, horrible, messed up by this administration.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
